pitome of order you've been  supplying to the military for many many  years going back to before World War two  we've made the sheepskin jackets bomber  jackets pea coats parkas we've made  police jackets for many different police  departments including the New York NYPD  and all of that is collective knowledge  that we apply into many of the jackets  we produce we're so busy focusing on on  the factory it's not just about function  and fashion for this clothing company  for us it's more important to stay true  to our heritage where we do best is  focusing on telling that story in our  production telling a story of Americana  through the styles we create rather than  focusing on what people are doing on  runways we're looking into our past and  that's where we get our inspiration  shots designs are timeless and so are  some of their production methods I've  always imagined that my perfecto was  made on the same machine that Brando's  and everybody else who wore their perfec  toes was made on we are using a lot of  the same machinery that we used from  those early days of in the 50s and even  before  if it ain't broke don't fix it keeping  the company domestic comes with a high  price tag but the tag the shot family  values most is the one sewn into every  jacket made in the USA I wouldn't want  to disappoint my great-grandfather and  my grandfather and be the one to take it  offshore I couldn't even imagine doing  that how do you think Jack and Irving  shot would feel about the company today  I think that they would be proud my  great-grandfather was very proud of  starting this company and he'd be very  proud to be here now it's up to the next  generation to keep this family affair up  and running you're the fourth generation  to run the company you have kids  are they the future I've shot I feel  very confident that there will be fifth  generation of family members that have  an interest in joining the business  because it's such a great place to work  I'd love co